--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` throws for a `#[Command]` parameter no argument's text converts into, rather than guessing — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's roster admits a subset of **one** enum's cases, and cases of two
// different enums are not that: there is no enum for an admitted word to be a
// case of, so no conversion can be chosen and
// `nvs_types::commands::ArgConv::Unconverted` is the honest row. An `#[Option]`
// declared at one is refused where it is written; a positional argument is the
// spelling that reaches a running program, because that compile error follows
// the marker rather than the type.
//
// The two wrong answers are a plausible first case and a usage error blaming
// the command line, so it is neither: a `LogicError`, which is what a mistake
// in the *program* is, since the table is fixed at compile time and no input
// can change what this row declares.
enum Level {
    Quiet,
    Loud,
}

enum Mode {
    Fast,
    Slow,
}

class Radio {
    #[Core\Command(name: "tune")]
    public static function tune(Level::Loud|Mode::Fast $level): uint {
        echo "tune ran\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

try {
    uint $status = Core\Command::run();
    echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--ARGS--
tune
Loud
--EXPECT--
`Core\Command::run` has no conversion into the type `tune`'s `level` is declared at
